module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.unary_minus_absorbs_a_following_additive_operator

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread, BlockerClassUnstated }

data unary_minus_absorbs_a_following_additive_operator: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "unary_minus_absorbs_a_following_additive_operator" as NonEmptyStr,
  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "**unary minus absorbs a following additive operator** (invalid state: the seed parser reads `-1 - j` as `-(1 - j)` and `-a + b` as `-(a + b)`. v1.compiler.parse parse_prefix parses the operand of ShMinus at min_bp 12, while extdeps.languages.dag.syntax gives infix `+` and `-` left_bp 13 (and `*`, `/` 15), so the operand loop keeps consuming every additive and multiplicative operator after the prefix. Harm: silent wrongness. The program is accepted and computes a different integer, with no diagnostic on any route that reads the seed's parse tree, which is the interpreter and every emitter.)",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: a negative literal standing alone (`last_rank: -1`, `{ -1 }`) is unaffected, as is any unary minus whose operand is followed by a comparison, a logical operator or a closing bracket. Only an additive or multiplicative infix operator that follows the operand is absorbed. A multiplicative absorption (`-a * b` read as `-(a * b)`) agrees in value except at the overflow boundary, so the additive case is the one that changes answers.",

    "RECEIPT 2026-10-19 (std.integer Int delivery 1): the signed grounding fold first spelled the negative encoding `-1 - j`, and `Negative { pred: 2 }` emitted `1` instead of `-3`. The emitted-bytes witness test.claim.integer_signed_grounding_emit_witness caught it, and a probe module confirmed the interpreter agrees with the emitter: `-1 - 2 == 0 - 3` is false. The fold now spells `0 - 1 - k`. A corpus census at that head, over every .dag file outside the stage0 mirrors, found no other expression of the exposed shape (a unary minus in expression-start position whose operand is followed by an arithmetic infix operator), validated against the known positives above.",

    "Rung found at: silent wrongness, which is outside the ladder and forbidden. It is latent today only because the census found no exposed site, and nothing stops the next author writing one. Ceiling: structurally guaranteed. Prefix binding power is a modeled grammar fact, and a prefix operand bound above every infix operator it should not absorb cannot misparse. Next trigger, at capability grain: prefix operators' binding powers declared in the language's syntax spec beside OperatorSpec (not the literal 12 in parse_prefix), read by the seed parser and by v2's grammar-row parse alike. A discriminating fixture must show `-1 - 2` evaluating to -3 on the interpreter and in the emitted Rust, and the v2 native parse must be checked for the same reading before either is called fixed.",

    "FIX RUNG 2026-10-20 (lane adhoc-9102e2d6-92f, PR #14559's successor): the trigger fired and was paid. std.syntax declares PrefixOperatorSpec { symbol, binding_power } beside OperatorSpec and SyntaxSpec carries prefix_operators; v1.compiler.parse parse_prefix reads BOTH its arms (ShBang and ShMinus, the same rule in a sibling arm per DESIGN 6b) through find_prefix_binding_power from ctx.env.syntax_spec -- the same table the infix gate reads -- and a symbol with no declared row is not a prefix operator there; no literal binding power survives in the parser. extdeps.languages.dag.syntax and extdeps.languages.rust.syntax (the only two SyntaxSpec constructors) declare `-` and `!` at 18: above `|>` (17), below `.` (19), so the prefix binds exactly its postfix operand -- the closure the v2 grammar rows enforce structurally (dag_grammar_unary_expr_expr: unary_expr -> '-' unary_expr | postfix_expr, which cannot reach an infix row at any power).",

    "EXECUTED EVIDENCE FOR THE FIX RUNG (all at the PR head, remote runner, one dispatch; the shape probes ran the two parsers directly and the value claim ran the interpreter over its own parsed body): (1) v2 owed AGREEMENT, not a re-plumb, and it was executed first -- the v2 shape probe (14 arms) is green on main unchanged, including the boundary probes '-a.b' (dot consumed INSIDE the operand by the postfix layer) and '-a |> f' (pipe kept OUTSIDE), which is what pinned the declared power at 18 rather than 20; the v1 shape probe reads 4 arms BAD on main (the RED: '-1 - 2' shaped as the prefix over the whole additive row) and 14 OK post-fix. (2) The v1-vs-v2 agreement claim runs the same texts through both parsers and requires identical nesting AND the independently-stated expected shape, defect cases and controls: -1 - 2, -(1 - 2), -a + b, -a * b, -1 < 0, standalone -3, !a && b, !a || b, !a * b, plain a - b, a * b, -a < b (twelve arms at the prefix rung; the postfix rung below extended the same claim to twenty-two measured arms). (3) The value claim evaluates on the v1 interpreter: -1 - 2 == -3, -1 + 2 == 1, !t && f == false, !t || t == true, the triple -1 - 2 - 3 == -6 (the old binding computes 4), controls -(1 - 2) == 1, -1 * 2 == -2, -1 < 0, -3 == 0 - 3; pre-fix the same claim refuses with -1 - 2 = 1 -- it is the RED. (4) The emitted-Rust channel, asserted with compile_dag_rust_emit_check poles that are CALIBRATED BY OBSERVATION (windowed dump, lane E7): Int operators route through runtime helpers, not literal -/: the fixed triple emits v1_rt::int_sub(v1_rt::int_sub(v1_rt::int_neg(1), 2), 3) (neg on the 1) and refuses it from the bug fixture, which emits v1_rt::int_neg(v1_rt::int_sub(v1_rt::int_sub(1, 2), 3)) (neg on the row); the bang emits ((!t.clone() && f.clone()) && t.clone()) against the bug's !((t.clone() && f.clone()) && t.clone()). Non-vacuity is executed: each bug-shaped fixture is emitted and matched, so the refuse-strings are real emissions, not hand-derived. (5) CENSUS AT MAIN, the silent-meaning-change ruling-out (manager msg_2153859d acceptance): the digest bin parses EVERY authored tree in the tree-wide corpus -- 174428 items over 9050 files -- under the OLD binding power (declared 18 patched to 12 in both language syntax mirrors) and the NEW declared power, with the skip counter LOUD (manager ruling: a silent skip hid two files from the first census): SKIPPED 1 at both bindings, the single skip named, src/v1/stage0/tests/fixtures/fact_cardinality_split_brace.dag, the fixture that exists to be unparseable (it carries no module header at all; the parser refuses it at 1:1). The digest diff at the re-spelled corpus is EMPTY (0 diff-lines, 0 non-fixture): every re-spell kept its meaning, which is the census' purpose. The first census (jc1a7cd3c, at the pre-re-spell corpus) differed at exactly 8 items -- the 7 corpus items carrying the 10 re-spelled sites plus this row's own value fixture -- which is what selected the re-spells. A grep candidate list of 43 lines was 38 already-parenthesized false positives (slice tests are blind to parens), so the tree digest, not the grep, is the census authority. (6) The seed mirror is regenerated with claim_executor --regen-round-cost over the present files, the round diff carries parse_prefix's declared-power read, and round 2 writes 0 files.",

    "POSTFIX RUNG 2026-10-21 (side-chat REQUEST_CHANGES item 3, same PR): a review showed the prefix half alone left the two parsers DISAGREEING on cast nesting -- with the prefix declared at 18 but `as` still an undeclared literal 13 in try_postfix, `-a as T` flipped to (-a) as T in v1 while v2's closed postfix production kept -(a as T), and `a + b as T` kept v1 reading the cast over the whole row where v2 reads it over the right operand. The same rule was therefore paid in the sibling arms: std.syntax declares PostfixOperatorSpec { symbol, binding_power } beside the prefix rows, both language specs declare ( at 21, [ at 21, as at 19, { at 13 (the brace row reproduces the old `14 <= min_bp` refusal exactly on the consume-iff scale), and every postfix gate in try_postfix and the no-brace loop now reads declared_postfix_gate_admits from ctx.env.syntax_spec.postfix_operators -- no literal gate threshold survives anywhere. `as` at 19 (above the prefix rows at 18, below call/index at 21) is the alignment choice: a unary operand consumes its cast on BOTH parsers, and an infix right operand consumes its cast on both (v2's reading, which v2 has always produced). The seed mirrors were regenerated over the merged sources by the round to a fixed point (BuildBuddy invocation b32bc874-796d-4941-b64d-81e561173864: both rounds rc=0 on regen-round-cost/heal/seed-regen, round 2 wrote 0; witnesses in the regenerated mirror: find_prefix_binding_power 3 reads, declared_postfix_gate_admits 8 reads, 0 literal gates, 4 PostfixOperatorSpec rows per language).",

    "CAST CENSUS (the silent-meaning-change ruling-out for the `as` row, DESIGN 5: an uncounted meaning change is a hard reject): the tree digest ran the FULL corpus (175291 items over 9084 files, SKIPPED 1 at both bindings -- the same named unparseable fixture, src/v1/stage0/tests/fixtures/fact_cardinality_split_brace.dag, refused at 1:1 with no module header; its PREFIX-token count is 0 so no prefix-operator site is excluded, verified by executing the host tokenizer over it, BuildBuddy invocation 749890d7-e4bb-47e3-bddf-a464e4d94af0) under the historical powers (prefix 12, as 13) and the declared powers (18/19). The diff is EXACTLY 12 authored items plus this row's own fixture: bed_partition.dag#5 process_allowance, bed_partition.dag#9 allocate_axis, cassette.dag#24 panel, cassette.dag#25 rear_socket, cassette.dag#30 guard, cassette.dag#36 rear_socket_bolt_clearances, rear_cooling.dag#13 rear_cooling_json, rear_fan_assembly.dag#10 guard, rear_fan_assembly.dag#11 fan_body, stack_geometry.dag#7 stack_pitch, stack_geometry.dag#10 pillar_height, stack_geometry.dag#14 stack_pitch_for_cooler. Every one is the same shape, an addition whose right operand is cast -- `... + f(...) as Int` -- and every cast is Nat-to-Int over a count: the two nestings evaluate identically for these sites, and v2 has ALWAYS parsed them the new way, so no authored corpus file changes MEANING against the parser it actually compiles with; the re-spell that would force the old nesting would move BOTH parsers to a shape neither produces today. That is the reason they are not re-spelled, stated rather than assumed.",

    "THE PER-ARM REDS, PUBLISHED AS MEASURED (correcting earlier drafts of this row and the PR body; an earlier draft claimed five value REDs and bang `&&`/`||` REDs -- the bang arms were NEVER reds: `&&`/`||` carry left_bp 7/5, below the old gate 12, so `(!a) && b` was the parse under both bindings, and the claim now records them as controls). Each channel's RED requires patching the table ITS OWN parse route reads; patching the other leaves it green (D5, invocation 7bcdbdcb-b997-48e0-ae20-35fb2d06c1ce: the agreement claim stayed rc=0 under a compiled-mirror patch because its v1 side evaluates dag_parse_environment from the .dag source; the values claim stayed rc=0 under the .dag patch because its arms are parsed by the compiled seed parser). (A) VALUE channel, historical rows patched into the compiled language mirrors and rebuilt (D2, invocation 83b6b361-8dd2-475e-9aba-1e165f8c6c15): unary_prefix_value_arms_hold refuses with `-1 - 2 = 1 (want -3); -1 + 2 = -3 (want 1); -1 - 2 - 3 = 4 (want -6)` -- exactly three failing arms -- while `!t && f = false (want false)` and `!t || t = true (want true)` report CORRECT under the old binding, and all controls (`-(1-2)==1`, `-1*2==-2`, `-1<0`, `-3==0-3`) hold. (B) SHAPE channel, historical rows patched into the .dag language specs and rebuilt (D6, invocation 01d7e71a-245a-43f8-b058-abd7ba930421 -- re-runnable via the lane scripts on the WIP branch): the 22-arm agreement claim refuses with exactly 8 DISAGREE arms -- -1 - 2, -a + b, -a * b, !a * b (the prefix absorbing the infix row, v1=P(I(l,l)) against v2=I(P(l),l)), a + b as T (v1=C(I(l,l)) against v2=I(l,C(l))), -a |> f and !a |> f (v1=P(M(l)) against v2=M(P(l))), and -a.b + c (v1=P(I(F(l),l)) against v2=I(P(F(l)),l)) -- and 14 arms OK, the controls holding under both bindings (-a as T = P(C(l)) under both, because the old prefix gate at 12 already stopped the cast row at 13... the cast stayed inside the operand under BOTH the old pairing (prefix 12, as-gate 13) and the new one (18/19), which is exactly why 19 -- not 13 re-declared -- is the value that keeps v1 and v2 together once the prefix moved above the infix rows). With the declared rows at 18/19 the same claim is rc=0 at the PR head, all arms agreeing and matching the independently stated expected shapes (22 at that rung; 32 after the record rung below).",

    "RECORD RUNG 2026-10-22 (dashboard review 84349, same PR): the declared postfix rows had a second sibling the prefix half exposed. A record literal under a unary operand -- `-Foo.Bar { x: 1 }` -- was bound INSIDE the operand by the old parser (prefix min_bp 12 made the brace gate's old rule `14 <= min_bp` decline to refuse) and by v2, whose grammar glues the record suffix at PRIMARY level (dag.dag dag_grammar_primary_ident_suffix_expr: optional(call-suffix | record-suffix), the tightest tier); but with the brace row declared at 13 -- the literal transcription of that old refusal edge onto the consume-iff scale -- the gate now REFUSED the brace at the prefix's min_bp 18, splitting the statement into `(-Foo.Bar)` plus a stray block: the same silent-reshape class, moved by a row instead of a literal. The plain family (`-Foo { x: 1 }`) was never at risk: `Foo {` is consumed earlier, by parse_ident_expr's UNGATED uppercase-ident + LBrace branch, so the declared gate only decides the DOTTED family (try_postfix's ExprFieldAccess brace arm) -- measured twelve cells x both routes x both bindings before the fix (lane invocations j504c81eb / j8a1e553b, per-arm strings in the PR body). FIX: the brace row is declared at 18 in both language specs -- v2's primary-level glue is the authority (manager ruling: one declared precedence both parsers read); 18 is >= the pipe's right power, below the dot (19) and call/index (21), reproduces the old arm at every position where the old parser saw text, and moves the three infix-right positions (additive 14, multiplicative 16, pipe 18) from v1's historical refusal onto v2's always-consume, where the census finds ZERO authored sites (4 regex hits over the .dag tree: one real, `SpanLocation.Unlocated {` at 00_core.dag:2853, parsed at min_bp 0 and unmoved; three in strings/comments). Control-flow braces never consult this row: the no-brace loop stops at `{` (parse_expr_loop_no_brace), so `match Foo {` / `if Foo.Bar {` parse as before. The two comments claiming preservation (both language specs' brace row, written against 13) were false at 18 and are corrected. THE CLAIM MOVED WITH THE ROW: the agreement claim gains ten record arms (32 total; the v1 canon lib gains ExprRecordLit/FieldInit arms printing F(F(l)) for one field, mirroring v2's CONJ-spine lowering -- a record that collapsed to a leaf could not carry this defect). Discriminating by EXECUTION, three channels: with ONLY the brace row patched 18 -> 13 (everything else declared), the claim fails on exactly the four dotted arms and every other arm stays green; at the full faithful-historical rows (12/12/13/13 in both specs AND both stage0 mirrors, binaries rebuilt between bindings -- a mirror-only or .dag-only patch re-measures the declared binding, the trap that voided the first receipt take), ten arms fail and the seed decode suite fails (its WireMigration arm carries the declared 21/21/19/18); and the full-corpus digest (178,072 items / 9,183 files / SKIPPED 1, the same named fixture) differs between bindings ONLY by the banked cast items plus the patched spec rows themselves, with a four-fixture dotted-record control corpus that DOES diff -- so the zero corpus diff cannot come from the digest being blind to the family.",

    "NAMED SINGLE-AUTHORITY DEBT left by this rung: TWO operator authorities still exist -- v1 reads SyntaxSpec.prefix_operators on the 5..20 scale (extdeps.languages.dag.syntax), v2 reads its grammar-row binding powers on the 1..7 scale (extdeps.languages.dag dag_infix_row). They agree where it matters (this row's executed agreement claim is the guard), but one table on one scale is the ceiling; the trigger for paying it is the day the two tables must move together (an operator added to one and not the other starts failing the agreement claim, which names this row). The direction of derivation is STATED (manager ruling, msg_bd71f326 forwarding wise-raven-693's finding): v2's grammar rows are the older authority for v2's own closed productions -- v2's unary and cast productions are structurally closed and cannot misread prefix or cast scope at any row value -- and v1's new rows were DERIVED to match the meanings v2's productions have always produced (prefix above every infix row and above the pipe, cast tightest under the prefix, call/index tightest of all). wise-raven-693's alternative prefix value 17 (equal to the pipe's left_bp) is REJECTED with its measured reason: v1's infix loop continues while left_bp >= min_bp, so a prefix at 17 parses its operand at 17 and a pipe inside the operand SURVIVES the gate -- `-a |> f` re-binds to -(a |> f), moving the absorption class from additive to pipe (measured: v1=P(M(l)) against the agreed M(P(l))); 18 is the least declared value that keeps the pipe outside the operand. The rows are still two tables; the agreement claim is the executed guard until one table subsumes the other.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
